# Make a monster's damage-equipment attack fail its save, and read the line
# the player is shown (bd inc-473).
#
#   INCURSION_DEQU_FORCE_SAVE=1 INCURSION_BIN=./incursion-dequ \
#   INCURSION_OPTIONS=tools/gates/Options.Dat \
#       tools/headless.sh tools/keys/dequ-save-message.keys 5
#
# WHAT IS BEING MEASURED. A_DEQU is a response attack: it fires from inside the
# resolution of the PLAYER's blow (src/Fight.cpp, the is_response_attk loop),
# not on the monster's own turn, so Freeze Monsters does not suppress it. When
# the player makes the Reflex save the handler prints a message and returns.
# That message is the oracle. Before the fix it read "The <item> protects its
# <item>." and went to every player EXCEPT the one whose sword was saved; after
# it, the character himself is told "You avoid harm to your <item>."
#
# WHY THE SAVE IS FORCED. The brown pudding's A_DEQU is DC 19 and the message
# only prints on a success, so an unforced script would be waiting on a die.
# The probe build (-DDEQU_PROBE) reads INCURSION_DEQU_FORCE_SAVE and takes the
# success branch; it changes which branch runs and nothing inside it, and the
# ordinary binary does not contain the test at all.
#
# WHY A BROWN PUDDING. It is one of the two monsters in lib/ that carry A_DEQU
# with a save DC (lib/mon3.irh:2770), and it is live: lib/program.i:45870 shows
# it as "60 for 5d6 57 (DC 19)", A_DEQU expanded to 60 and AD_DCAY to 57.
#
# Freeze Monsters is on so the pudding neither moves nor takes a turn of its
# own. It still answers a blow, which is the whole point.

a       # Initial Choices: [a] Create a New Character
n       # Alter the character generation options? -- no
y       # Explore Mode -- yes
i       # Race:  [i] Orc
a       # Subrace: [a] Orc (Standard)
j       # Class: [j] Warrior -- starts with a weapon in hand
a       # Attributes: the first offered set
c       # Alignment: [c] Neutral
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Focus on which Weapon" a
@while "|  *" RIGHT RIGHT RIGHT DOWN
ENTER y
@while "wish to worship" y
@while "Choose a Domain" a
@while "|  *" RIGHT RIGHT RIGHT DOWN
ENTER y
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Focus on which Weapon" a
@while "|  *" RIGHT RIGHT RIGHT DOWN
ENTER y
a       # Personality archetype
@until "Things in View" ESC

# 'w' opens wizard mode and 'y' confirms it the first time. The pair is driven
# by @until rather than written out, because a keystroke swallowed by a prompt
# that was still up would spend every key after it on the wrong thing, and that
# is exactly what happened on the first attempt at this script.
@until "Wizard Mode Options" w y
w       # [w] Wizard Mode Switches
# The switch list draws no cursor a screen dump can see, so this counts rows:
# Freeze Monsters is the eleventh, and the cursor opens on the first.
DOWN*10
SPACE   # Freeze Monsters: NO -> YES
ESC

@until "Wizard Mode Options" w
@choose "Monster Summoning"
@choose "Jellies"
@choose "brown pudding"
RIGHT ENTER
@dump:pudding-summoned

# Walk into it. Each bump is one melee attack. A miss prints no save message,
# so swing several times; the pudding is frozen and cannot walk away.
RIGHT
@dump:strike-1
RIGHT
@dump:strike-2
RIGHT
@dump:strike-3
RIGHT
@dump:strike-4
RIGHT
@dump:strike-5
RIGHT
@dump:strike-6

@quit
